    <div class="tile" style="--x:76%;--y:2%;--r:-4deg;--d:-4s;--g1:#f6d365;--g2:#fda085"><b></b><i></i><span>♥ 312K</span></div>
    <div class="tile" style="--x:-9%;--y:81%;--r:6deg;--d:-1s;--g1:#fbc2eb;--g2:#a6c1ee"><b></b><i></i><span>♥ 9.8K</span></div>
    <div class="tile" style="--x:38%;--y:88%;--r:-3deg;--d:-3s;--g1:#d4fc79;--g2:#96e6a1"><b></b><i></i><span>♥ 77K</span></div>
    <div class="tile" style="--x:77%;--y:80%;--r:8deg;--d:-5s;--g1:#ffecd2;--g2:#fcb69f"><b></b><i></i><span>♥ 2.4M</span></div>
  </div>
  <div class="col">
    <p class="eyebrow">An essay in six depths and one ascent</p>
    <h1 class="title"><span>Mimics</span> <em>&amp; Mythmakers</em></h1>
    <div class="reflection" aria-hidden="true"><span>Mimics</span> <em>&amp; Mythmakers</em></div>
    <p class="deck">Every man is a mirror. Most of them are pointed at a screen.</p>
    <p class="byline">Michael Wilhelm</p>
    <p class="cue">Scroll to go under</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="zone water day" data-depth="0,0" data-ink="day" style="--from:#e2edef;--to:#bcd6dc">
  <div class="col flow">
    <header class="zone-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The surface · 0 m</p>
      <p class="glyph" lang="grc">Ἠχώ</p>
      <p class="gloss"><i>Ēchō</i> · the sound that comes back</p>
      <h2>The Mirror</h2>
    </header>
    <p class="lead">Men are mimics. We learn to be men by mirroring other men, years before we know we're doing it. The walk. The laugh. The way your father set his jaw when he was angry. It lives in you before you can spell it.</p>
    <p>Psychologists showed it with a puzzle box. Show a young chimpanzee and a three-year-old how to get a treat out, and throw in a few pointless moves: slide a bolt for no reason, tap the lid with a stick. Make the box see-through, so anyone can tell those moves do nothing. The chimp skips them. The child copies every single one.</p>
    <p>Scientists call it over-imitation. We are the animal that copies the useless steps too. It's how a culture survives a thousand years. It's also how idiocracy spreads in an afternoon.</p>
    <p>So the stories leaders tell carry real weight. The depth of the myth has to touch equally deep parts of the souls of the men matching it.</p>
    <p class="pull">Look at what we're matching now. Meme pages give us our dopamine. Leaders give us idiocracy, with orders. And people follow.</p>
    <p>The Greeks already told this story. Echo was a mountain nymph cursed so she could never speak first. All she could say were the last few words someone else had just said to her.<span class="echo" aria-hidden="true"> <span>said to her.</span> <span>to her.</span> <span>her.</span></span></p>
    <p>She fell for Narcissus, a beautiful boy who could only love the face he found on the surface of a pool. Ancient writers heard <i>narkē</i> (<span class="gk" lang="grc">νάρκη</span>) in his name. Numbness. It's the root of our word <i>narcotic</i>.</p>
    <p>In Ovid's telling, Echo wastes away until only her voice is left. Narcissus wastes away at the edge of the pool. She repeats. He stares. Neither one ever goes in.</p>
    <p>Meet the pick-me man. Echo with a phone. He mimics the memes and the energy of a page, then pines after the woman behind it, like the page and the woman are the same thing.</p>
    <p class="pull">Shallow parasociality. Low vibe stories create low vibe men.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="waterline" data-depth="0,40" data-ink="split">
  <svg class="wave" viewBox="0 0 1200 40" preserveAspectRatio="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M0 20 Q75 6 150 20 T300 20 T450 20 T600 20 T750 20 T900 20 T1050 20 T1200 20 V40 H0 Z" fill="#1d6d84"/></svg>
  <p class="wl-text"><span class="wl-a">So go in.</span><span class="wl-b" aria-hidden="true">So go in.</span></p>
</div>

<section class="zone water night" data-depth="40,200" data-ink="night" style="--from:#1d6d84;--to:#134a5e">
  <div class="col flow">
    <header class="zone-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Sunlight zone · 0–200 m</p>
      <p class="glyph" lang="grc">ἐπί</p>
      <p class="gloss"><i>epi</i> · upon</p>
      <h2>Borrowed Light</h2>
      <p class="note">Oceanographers named every layer of the open sea in Greek. This one is the epipelagic, upon the open sea.</p>
    </header>
    <p>Up here, life runs on borrowed light. The sun hits the water, plankton turn it into food, and nearly everything in the shallows lives off that one source.</p>
    <p class="lead">This is where the mimic lives. Warm, lit, and running on somebody else's sun.</p>
    <p>Mimics are men ignorant of their own idiocratic nature. Some of them are avoiding something. Pain. Grief. Shame. Fear. The shallows feel safe when the deep is holding what you're running from.</p>
    <p>Others are comfortable here. Nostalgic about the myths they've heard. They quote the heroes, wear the heroes, argue about the heroes in the comments, and stay unaware that they, too, could make meaning like the stories they cope with.</p>
    <p class="pull">Every story a mimic leans on was made by a man who went deeper than the mimic is willing to go.</p>
    <p>The shallows keep one secret. Every hero and every villain you ever followed was just a dude in a costume. He made the costume himself. So can you.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="zone water night" data-depth="200,1000" data-ink="night" style="--from:#134a5e;--to:#0a2a39">
  <div class="col flow">
    <header class="zone-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Twilight zone · 200–1,000 m</p>
      <p class="glyph" lang="grc">μέσος</p>
      <p class="gloss"><i>mesos</i> · middle</p>
      <h2>Destiny Swappers</h2>
    </header>
    <p>The light thins out here, a little more every meter. Some sunlight still reaches this far, enough to see shapes and nowhere near enough to live on.</p>
    <p>Every night, the twilight zone launches the largest migration on Earth. Fish, squid and krill rise in the dark to feed near the surface, then sink back down before sunrise. Up all night, feeding on the surface, gone by morning. Sound like anyone you know?</p>
    <p class="lead">Some of them come up to feed on you.</p>
    <p>Mimics can become things that mirror your story back at you with the intention of stealing it, or quietly altering it. Destiny swappers, they're also called.</p>
    <p>The Greeks used one word for both halves of this, <i>zēlos</i> (<span class="gk" lang="grc">ζῆλος</span>). It meant wanting to be like a man, and it meant wanting what he has. Emulation and envy in the same breath.</p>
    <p>René Girard spent a career on that knot. We borrow our desires from the people we watch. When two men want the same thing, imitation curdles into rivalry.</p>
    <p>A swapper studies what you want, then wants it louder. He hands your reflection back with a few edits, and if you aren't anchored in your own depth, you'll take the edited copy and call it yours.</p>
    <p class="pull">Every idea lives in the collective unconscious. If the man it shows up for won't use it, it finds someone who will. Swappers feed on destinies left floating in the shallows.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="zone water night" data-depth="1000,4000" data-ink="night" style="--from:#0a2a39;--to:#04121c">
  <div class="col flow">
    <header class="zone-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Midnight zone · 1,000–4,000 m</p>
      <p class="glyph" lang="grc">βαθύς</p>
      <p class="gloss"><i>bathys</i> · deep</p>
      <h2>The Dive</h2>
    </header>
    <p>Past a thousand meters, the sun is gone for good. Whatever light you see down here, something alive made it.</p>
    <p>Researchers at the Monterey Bay Aquarium Research Institute went through seventeen years of deep-sea video, from the surface to almost four kilometers down. Three of every four animals they saw in the open water could make their own light. Their lab calls it almost certainly the most common way living things communicate on this planet.</p>
    <figure class="fig glow" style="--mark:var(--biolum);--track:rgba(127,208,255,.13)">
      <p class="fig-num">76<small>of every 100</small></p>
      <div class="units" data-n="76" role="img" aria-label="76 of 100 dots lit: animals observed in the open ocean that can make their own light"></div>
      <div class="key"><span>Can make light</span><span class="off">Can't</span></div>
      <figcaption>Animals seen in the open water off California, surface to 3,900 m. Martini &amp; Haddock, <i>Scientific Reports</i>, 2017.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="lead">Down here, the only light is the light you make.</p>
    <p class="hint">Tap the dark</p>
    <p class="lead">This is the work. Look around and feel the world around you. Reach deep and create the meaning in yourself. Test it. Dive deeper. Test it. Repeat.</p>
    <figure class="fig dive">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 360 232" role="img" aria-label="Dive profile: down to reach, up to test, deeper, up to test, deeper, up to test with others, then back to the dive">
        <line class="surf" x1="0" y1="28" x2="360" y2="28"/>
        <text class="lbl" x="0" y="18">surface</text>
        <text class="lbl" x="274" y="18" text-anchor="middle">test with others</text>
        <polyline class="path" points="10,28 58,98 94,58 146,134 182,76 236,166 274,38 350,198"/>
        <circle class="pt" cx="58" cy="98" r="4"/>
        <circle class="pt" cx="94" cy="58" r="4"/>
        <circle class="pt" cx="146" cy="134" r="4"/>
        <circle class="pt" cx="182" cy="76" r="4"/>
        <circle class="pt" cx="236" cy="166" r="4"/>
        <circle class="pt" cx="274" cy="38" r="4"/>
        <circle class="pt" cx="350" cy="198" r="4"/>
        <text class="lbl" x="58" y="120" text-anchor="middle">reach</text>
        <text class="lbl" x="94" y="46" text-anchor="middle">test</text>
        <text class="lbl" x="146" y="156" text-anchor="middle">deeper</text>
        <text class="lbl" x="182" y="64" text-anchor="middle">test</text>
        <text class="lbl" x="236" y="188" text-anchor="middle">deeper</text>
        <text class="lbl" x="352" y="222" text-anchor="end">back to the dive</text>
        <circle class="diver" r="3.5"><animateMotion dur="10s" repeatCount="indefinite" path="M10,28 L58,98 L94,58 L146,134 L182,76 L236,166 L274,38 L350,198"/></circle>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>A dive computer would log this as a yo-yo profile: down, up, down again, deeper each time. Divers get warned off it. Meaning makers don't get a choice.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Then you test the meaning out in the world. With other men. With women. With children. Much of the time, those tests send you right back to the dive. Good. The trip back down is part of the work.</p>
    <p>When the meaning holds and the myth is relatable, people form around it. Friends. Allies. Wives. Children. Tribes.</p>
    <p class="pull">The depth of the meaning is directly tied to the depth of the identity attached to it.</p>
    <p class="lead">Shallow meaning makes a costume. Deep meaning makes a man.</p>
    <p>Now look at the tribes. In 1990, 3 in 100 American men said they had no close friends. By 2021 it was 15 in 100.</p>
    <figure class="fig" style="--mark:var(--biolum);--track:rgba(127,208,255,.13)">
      <div class="pair">
        <div><p>1990</p><div class="units sm" data-n="3" role="img" aria-label="1990: 3 of 100 men reported no close friends"></div><p class="val">3 in 100</p></div>
        <div><p>2021</p><div class="units sm" data-n="15" role="img" aria-label="2021: 15 of 100 men reported no close friends"></div><p class="val">15 in 100</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="key"><span>No close friends</span><span class="off">At least one</span></div>
      <figcaption>American men. Survey Center on American Life, 2021, against a 1990 Gallup poll. The method changed between the two, and some later polls find a smaller gap.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="lead">Know the people around you. Know the color of their eyes.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="zone water night" data-depth="4000,6000" data-ink="night" style="--from:#04121c;--to:#01070c">
  <div class="col flow">
    <header class="zone-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The abyss · 4,000–6,000 m</p>
      <p class="glyph" lang="grc">ἄβυσσος</p>
      <p class="gloss"><i>abyssos</i> · bottomless</p>
      <h2>Blood Memory</h2>
    </header>
    <p>Four thousand meters down, the water presses in at about four hundred times the pressure at the surface. Anything built for the shallows gets crushed. Anything built for the deep holds its shape.</p>
    <p class="lead">Identity works the same way. So does a family line.</p>
    <p class="pull">Experiences live in our blood.</p>
    <p>Homer knew it. In the <i>Odyssey</i>, Odysseus digs a pit at the edge of the world and fills it with blood for the dead. His mother, who died of grief waiting for him to come home, sits beside it in silence, unable to look at her own son. She drinks. Then she knows him. Then she speaks.</p>
    <p>The science is younger and louder. People conceived during the Dutch Hunger Winter of 1944 still carried chemical marks on their genes six decades later. Whether marks like that travel further down a family line is still a live fight in biology.</p>
    <p>The reason it's so hard to settle is the best part. Researchers struggle to separate the biology from everything else a family hands down: habits, fears, the way a man carries himself, the stories. The story rides the bloodline so closely that science has trouble pulling the two apart.</p>
    <p class="pull">We have a duty to give our line a better story. A higher story.</p>
    <p>Go back to better times, the ones in our blood. Men rallied around leaders who grew the world for them, who gave them more to consider in their own depths about what their place in it was.</p>
    <p>Community ceremonies formed. Traditions formed. And so long as the world related to the society, and the leader mirrored it back accurately and justly, the society stuck together.</p>
    <p class="big">This is the path forward.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="zone water night" data-depth="6000,10935" data-ink="night" style="--from:#01070c;--to:#000000">
  <div class="col flow">
    <header class="zone-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Hadal zone · 6,000–10,935 m</p>
      <p class="glyph" lang="grc">ᾍδης</p>
      <p class="gloss"><i>Hadēs</i> · the unseen one</p>
      <h2>The Storyteller</h2>
    </header>
    <p>The deepest water on Earth is named for the underworld. Hadal, from Hades.</p>
    <p>The Greeks had a word for the hero's trip down there, <i>katabasis</i> (<span class="gk" lang="grc">κατάβασις</span>), the going-down. Orpheus went down for love. Heracles went down for a three-headed dog. Odysseus sailed to the edge of the dead to ask for directions home. The ones who came back up carried something the living couldn't get any other way.</p>
    <p class="lead">That's the storyteller. He brings it all together. The meaning maker. The one willing to reach the deepest inside himself, check what he finds against the world and against society, and then relate it, the widest and the deepest at once.</p>
    <p>The leader and the mimic both carry a mirror. The mimic points his at you, to take your story. The leader points his at the world and shows it back to you accurately and justly, so you can find your own place in it.</p>
    <p class="pull">A true leader's story sends you into your own dive. Orders are what a leader hands out when his story isn't deep enough to move you.</p>
    <p>For tens of thousands of years before anyone wrote a word down, the story was the archive, the law, the map and the medicine. A people that loses its stories forgets who it is. Someone else is always happy to tell it.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bottom water night" data-depth="10935,10935" data-ink="night" data-zone="Challenger Deep" style="--from:#000000;--to:#000000">
  <div class="col">
    <p class="eyebrow">10,935 m · Challenger Deep</p>
    <p class="big">The bottom.</p>
    <p class="lead">Now come up. Bring it with you.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="zone water night up" data-depth="10935,40" data-phase="up" data-ink="night" style="--from:#000000;--to:#3a2a22">
  <div class="col flow">
    <header class="zone-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">The ascent · 10,935 → 0 m</p>
      <p class="glyph" lang="grc">ἀνάβασις</p>
      <p class="gloss"><i>anabasis</i> · the going-up</p>
      <h2>The Way Up</h2>
    </header>
    <p class="lead">Coming back up, you see the surface for what it is.</p>
    <p>Plato described prisoners chained in place since childhood, facing a wall, watching shadows thrown by things carried along behind them. They handed out honors to whoever was quickest to name the passing shadows and best at guessing which would come next. Twenty-four centuries later, we put the cave in your pocket.</p>
    <p>When one of them got loose, saw the sun, and came back down to tell the others, they laughed at him. Plato wrote that if they could get their hands on whoever tried to free them, they'd kill him.</p>
    <p class="pull">People are strapped to a world that is going to break their minds if they can't keep up with an accurate story about reality.</p>
    <p class="lead">Look at what we're keeping up with.</p>
    <p>The average American spends close to seven hours a day on an internet-connected screen. That's nearly half the hours you're awake.</p>
    <figure class="fig" style="--mark:var(--dawn);--track:rgba(226,176,98,.17)">
      <p class="fig-num">≈7h<small>of a 16-hour waking day</small></p>
      <div class="meter" data-n="7" data-total="16" role="img" aria-label="About 7 of 16 waking hours on an internet-connected screen"></div>
      <div class="key"><span>On a screen</span><span class="off">Everything else</span></div>
      <figcaption>U.S. internet users, DataReportal figures compiled by Exploding Topics. Other estimates run from about 6½ to 7 hours.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>In 2004, 28% of Americans read for pleasure on an average day. By 2023 it was 16%, a drop of more than 40% in twenty years.</p>
    <figure class="fig" style="--mark:var(--dawn);--track:rgba(232,239,241,.3)">
      <div class="bars" role="img" aria-label="Americans reading for pleasure on an average day: 28 percent in 2004, 16 percent in 2023">
        <div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-yr">2004</span><span class="bar"><b class="past" style="--w:.933"></b><span class="bar-val">28%</span></span></div>
        <div class="bar-row"><span class="bar-yr">2023</span><span class="bar"><b style="--w:.533"></b><span class="bar-val">16%</span></span></div>
      </div>
      <figcaption>Share of Americans reading for pleasure on an average day. Bone et al., <i>iScience</i>, 2025, from the American Time Use Survey.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>By one widely cited Gallup analysis of federal data, 54 of every 100 American adults read below the equivalent of a sixth-grade level. The newest federal survey found the share at the very bottom of the scale climbed from 19% to 28% between 2017 and 2023.</p>
    <figure class="fig" style="--mark:var(--dawn);--track:rgba(226,176,98,.17)">
      <p class="fig-num">54<small>of every 100 adults</small></p>
      <div class="units" data-n="54" role="img" aria-label="54 of 100 dots filled: American adults reading below the equivalent of a sixth-grade level"></div>
      <div class="key"><span>Below a sixth-grade equivalent</span><span class="off">At or above</span></div>
      <figcaption>Adults 16–74. Gallup for the Barbara Bush Foundation, 2020, from federal PIAAC data; the test's makers discourage grade-level comparisons. 2023 figures from NCES.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p>Language is the operating system. The way we speak wires the brain, and words become actions. Dumb down a people's language and you undermine the civilization running on it.</p>
    <p>So the storyteller's job just got harder, and it matters more. Take the deepest thing you know and carry it down to where a child could understand it, without losing any of the depth on the way.</p>
    <p>Millennials are the last generation to remember life before constant connection. That memory is a job. Distill what mattered and hand it to the kids being raised on AI.</p>
    <p class="pull">And the accurate story about reality probably isn't one another guy told you.</p>
    <p>Your own mind is your most important possession. If you believe something only because you were told it, peel back a few layers and look at what's underneath.</p>
    <p>Find it for yourself before history gets rewritten again. Orwell gave his Party the slogan for it: who controls the past controls the future.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="surfacing" data-depth="40,0" data-phase="up" data-ink="split" style="--from:#3a2a22;--to:#e4c48c" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<section class="final water day" data-depth="0,0" data-phase="up" data-ink="day" style="--from:#e4c48c;--to:#f1ddb3">
  <div class="col">
    <p class="eyebrow">The surface · 0 m</p>
    <p class="big">You make your own history every single day.</p>
    <p class="actlike">Act like it.</p>
    <button class="again" type="button" id="again" aria-label="Back to the top to dive again">Dive again <span aria-hidden="true">↑</span></button>
  </div>
</section>

<footer class="soundings water day" data-depth="0,0" data-phase="up" data-ink="day" style="--from:#f1ddb3;--to:#f5e7c8">
  <div class="col">
    <p class="eyebrow">Soundings</p>
    <h2 class="snd-h">Where the depth readings came from</h2>
    <ol class="snd">
      <li>Over-imitation: Victoria Horner and Andrew Whiten, “Causal knowledge and imitation/emulation switching in chimpanzees and children,” <i>Animal Cognition</i>, 2005. <a href="https://research-portal.st-andrews.ac.uk/en/publications/causal-knowledge-and-imitationemulation-switching-in-chimpanzees-/">St Andrews record</a></li>
      <li>Echo and Narcissus: Ovid, <i>Metamorphoses</i>, Book III. On <i>narkē</i> and the flower's name, including the view that the name is older than Greek: <a href="https://www.etymonline.com/word/Narcissus">Etymonline</a></li>
      <li>The nightly migration: <a href="https://twilightzone.whoi.edu/greatest-migration-on-earth-happens-under-darkness-every-day">Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution, Ocean Twilight Zone project</a></li>
      <li><i>Zēlos</i> and mimetic desire: René Girard, <i>Deceit, Desire and the Novel</i>, 1961.</li>
      <li>Bioluminescence: Séverine Martini and Steven Haddock, <i>Scientific Reports</i> 7:45750, 2017 (<a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5379559/">paper</a>); MBARI on light as communication (<a href="https://www.mbari.org/project/bioluminescence-and-fluorescence/">project page</a>).</li>
      <li>Close friends: Survey Center on American Life, American Perspectives Survey, 2021, summarized alongside later polls by the <a href="https://aibm.org/research/male-loneliness-and-isolation-what-the-data-shows/">American Institute for Boys and Men</a>.</li>
      <li>The blood pit: Homer, <i>Odyssey</i>, Book 11.</li>
      <li>The Hunger Winter: Bastiaan Heijmans et al., “Persistent epigenetic differences associated with prenatal exposure to famine in humans,” <i>PNAS</i>, 2008. <a href="https://fermatslibrary.com/s/persistent-epigenetic-differences-associated-with-prenatal-exposure-to-famine-in-humans">Annotated copy</a></li>
      <li>Why it's hard to settle: Bernhard Horsthemke, “A critical view on transgenerational epigenetic inheritance in humans,” <i>Nature Communications</i>, 2018. <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6065375">Paper</a></li>
      <li>The cave: Plato, <i>Republic</i>, Book VII, 514a–517a.</li>
      <li>Screen time: <a href="https://explodingtopics.com/blog/screen-time-stats">DataReportal figures compiled by Exploding Topics</a>.</li>
      <li>Reading for pleasure: Jessica Bone, Feifei Bu, Jill Sonke and Daisy Fancourt, <i>iScience</i>, 2025. <a href="https://www.ucl.ac.uk/news/2025/aug/proportion-americans-reading-pleasure-fell-40-over-20-years">UCL summary</a></li>
      <li>Literacy: Gallup for the Barbara Bush Foundation, 2020, from federal PIAAC data (<a href="https://www.snopes.com/news/2022/08/02/us-literacy-rate/">Snopes check</a>); the caution on grade-level comparisons (<a href="https://apmresearchlab.org/10x-adult-literacy">APM Research Lab</a>); 2023 results (<a href="https://ies.ed.gov/learn/press-release/u-s-adults-score-par-international-average-literacy-skills-below-international-average-numeracy-and">NCES</a>).</li>
      <li>George Orwell, <i>Nineteen Eighty-Four</i>, 1949.</li>
      <li>Depths follow the standard ocean zones. Challenger Deep is usually given as about 10,935 m; estimates differ by a few dozen meters.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mm-foot"><span>an essay &middot; Michael Wilhelm &middot; 2026</span><a href="/gallery/">Return to the gallery</a></p>
  </div>
</footer>

</main>
</Poem>

